{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's mechanistic account is non-causal as specified: it asserts that rival primitives are 'artificially excluded from the action space,' but it never identifies the causal structure by which ownership allocation performs this exclusion. The problem demands a causal mechanism connecting the ownership rule to suppression of cross-cutting explanations; the target instead redescribes the effect (search space pruned, cross-cutting explanations unrepresentable) as if it were the mechanism. A genuine causal mechanism would specify the step-by-step procedure by which the lineage-ownership rule prevents rival primitives from entering the evaluation/action space (e.g., a routing function that discards non-owner tokens before evaluation, or a cost function that assigns infinite cost to non-owner primitives). Because the target conflates the outcome ('unrepresentable in the allocated subspace') with the process that produces it, it fails to supply the causal mechanism the problem explicitly requires.","counterexample":["A causal mechanism: 'When a problem p with parent artifact A is allocated, the system calls Allocate(p) := Lineage(A); the dispatcher enforces a whitelist filter W(Lineage(A)) on the primitive vocabulary, so any primitive token t not in W is rejected at the parse stage before search begins; this parse-stage rejection is the causal step that removes cross-cutting explanations from the action space.' The target lacks any analogous step-level specification, offering only the endpoint description that rival primitives are excluded.","Against the target's mechanism text: the statement 'rival primitives ... are artificially excluded from the action space' is the conclusion to be causally explained, not the explanation itself; without an intervening causal device (filter, router, cost penalty), the mechanism is circular."],"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}